WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.520
We are in the middle of one of the most interesting
stock market related stories that I can remember

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.450
in a really long time, and when I say we're
in the middle of it, we are really in the

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:10.450
middle of it.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.820
So I hope to do justice to what's gone on
so far.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:20.929
And the way I'm describing this is a game
stop short squeeze that is potentially going

00:00:20.929 --> 00:00:23.839
to nuke some hedge funds.

00:00:23.839 --> 00:00:27.279
And let's just start at the beginning.

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:32.760
Companies exist and some of them are publicly
traded, meaning you can buy shares in those

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:33.760
companies.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.590
I know I'm going very, very basic here.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:37.960
One such company is GameStop.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:43.550
GameStop is a chain of stores where you can
buy video games.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:49.320
And of course, with the advent of buying video
games online and having them delivered digitally

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:57.600
through, for example, steam, a service that
is available to download games, GameStop had

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.950
been struggling for some time.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:05.379
Coronaviruses then hit, which dramatically
decrease the number of people that were doing

00:01:05.379 --> 00:01:09.690
in-person shopping, especially for something
like video games when you can just get them

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.790
on the Internet already.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:19.460
So GameStop stock started to decline and this
led to a lot of people and subsequently hedge

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.280
funds short selling the stock.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.909
Now, what does it mean to short sell a stock?

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:30.530
Again, we're not going to do an hour on short
selling, but the gist of it is as follows.

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:36.270
The way most of us think about investing in
stocks for what we might call the long term

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:41.999
is that we are long on a stock, meaning you
buy it at a certain price.

00:01:41.999 --> 00:01:43.820
The plan is to hold it.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.649
Hopefully the price goes up and then you sell
the stock if you buy it.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:54.149
Fifty dollars a share sell at a hundred dollars
a share, you make fifty dollars per share,

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:56.340
but you can do essentially the reverse.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.799
You can short sell a stock.

00:01:58.799 --> 00:02:05.460
And what that means is you are placing a bet
that the stock goes down rather than up.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:11.110
And instead of first doing the buying and
then doing the selling, you are first selling

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:16.090
borrowed stock and then you are repurchasing
it in the future.

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:21.240
And with that same 50 and 100 dollar number
in reverse, you can make fifty dollars per

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.240
share.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.670
If there is a stock trading now at one hundred
dollars per share that I believe in the future

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.460
will trade at 50.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:34.030
I can borrow and sell shares for a hundred
dollars.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:40.510
When it drops to 50, I buy them for fifty
dollars and now I've made fifty dollars per

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.920
share.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.470
The risk is there is only so much a stock
can go down zero.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:52.620
Meaning if you invest a thousand dollars that
could end up being worth zero and you're out

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:59.620
a thousand dollars when you short sell, there's
really no limit to how much you can lose.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:06.020
You can borrow shares and if they go from
a thousand to 2000 dollars, you've lost a

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:07.170
thousand dollars.

00:03:07.170 --> 00:03:12.050
But if they go from one thousand to three
thousand dollars, you've now lost two thousand

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.050
dollars.

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.440
And as you can see, there's no limit to the
amount that you can lose.

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:22.350
Indeed, what took place is that a bunch of
different hedge funds and big investors were

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:26.020
certain of the demise of GameStop stock.

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.960
They shorted it.

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:34.170
And A now this is where they start to be different
opinions about exactly the sequence.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:42.050
But the long and short of it is that some
online enclaves where stock trades are discussed,

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:47.210
including a sub Reddit called Wall Street
Bets, pointed out, hey, look at this.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:52.160
There are a lot of shorts here that could
potentially be squeezed.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:56.640
I am going to start buying and buying and
buying this stock.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:59.110
That is exactly what tons of people did.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:06.110
And the the price of the stock exploded, meaning
that these hedge funds are now very upside

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.110
down.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:12.830
Soon, many of these hedge funds are going
to remember your first selling and then buying

00:04:12.830 --> 00:04:19.120
when you short, they are going to have to
buy and they are going to have to buy at way

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.540
more than they sold, putting them underwater
and in fact, putting some hedge funds, as

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:30.190
far as we are being told, at risk of insolvency,
at risk of going out of business.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.700
A few interesting takes on this.

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:40.210
Congresswoman Alexandria Cassio Cortez tweeted,
Got to admit, it's really something to see

00:04:40.210 --> 00:04:46.900
Wall Streeters with a long history of treating
our economy as a casino, complain about a

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:47.900
message.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:51.390
Board of posters also treating the market
as a casino.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:54.830
Our friend Anna Kasparian from the Young Turks
tweeting, tweeting.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:58.490
So market manipulation by Federal Reserve
pumping money into.

00:04:58.490 --> 00:05:01.430
Failing banks and corporations is OK.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:05.930
But Reddit users rallying GameStop is wrong
and must be regulated.

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.770
The entire stock market is disconnected from
reality.

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:12.760
Funny how quickly the financial press cries
for hedge funds.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.770
So here's my take on this.

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:22.260
This run up of the stock price of GameStop
is no more wrong than the other types of speculation,

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.810
including the shorting that others do just
because it's being organized on Reddit instead

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:31.230
of by hedge funds high up in skyscrapers in
Washington.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:34.440
New York City doesn't make it any more right
or wrong.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:39.140
What this does expose, again, in case you
forgot, is that the stock market is not the

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.150
economy.

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:46.321
And so much like when we say, oh, stocks are
up, but it's hedge funds making money, that's

00:05:46.321 --> 00:05:48.180
not really the economy.

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:52.350
The posters on Reddit that are doing this
now, making a bunch of money, it's also not

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.350
the economy.

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.090
It just reminds us the stock market is not
the economy.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:01.270
Now, these hedge funds made risky deals.

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:05.180
And if the stock market if the stock price
kept declining, they would have made a bunch

00:06:05.180 --> 00:06:06.180
of money.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.180
It went up.

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:09.620
So now they're at risk of losing money.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:13.780
There are now a bunch of hedge funds that
are saying this isn't OK and we need regu

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:15.620
this shouldn't be allowed to happen.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.940
Well, hold on a second.

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.720
If you want to do risky things, it's risk
reward.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.850
The possibility of a big reward comes with
risk.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:28.060
You can't now say you want the big reward,
but you want the risk taken off the table

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:29.580
by the government.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:33.130
They are saying it, but it is extraordinarily
hypocritical.

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:38.800
Now, there's a really important aspect to
this, which is that many of the commentaries,

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:45.750
the hot takes are people debating whether
the Wall Street bets, posters who are running

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:51.780
up the price of the stock are sincerely buying
GameStop stock because they believe it's a

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:57.889
good company with good prospects or whether
they are ironically buying GameStop stock

00:06:57.889 --> 00:07:01.180
or cynically buying the stock.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:02.500
That doesn't matter.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.630
That could not be less relevant.

00:07:04.630 --> 00:07:06.400
That's what hedge funds do.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.830
Do you think that hedge funds and other investors
are saying we only buy long or short, sell

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:20.850
a stock based on our sincerely held deep down
beliefs about the ethics and business prospects

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.630
and management of the company?

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.610
No, they're making speculative bets either
way.

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:30.850
And the idea about, oh, these aren't genuine
pumpers, what difference does it make?

00:07:30.850 --> 00:07:36.170
That's the whole point of many of the people
who have been saying the behavior by the hedge

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:38.900
funds is predatory and cynical.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:43.780
Now they are saying, well, no, no, no, the
Wall Street bets people are the cynical ones

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:49.880
who thought to apply that to when Kelly Lefler,
senator, former senator from Georgia, made

00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:54.740
those stock moves once she was briefed about
what was happening and what was incoming with

00:07:54.740 --> 00:08:00.500
the pandemic, was she saying, oh, I don't
think the management of these companies, you

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:07.540
know, she predicted that there were going
to be general shocks to the system that would

00:08:07.540 --> 00:08:10.300
make these shares be worthless.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.009
And so she decided to sell.

00:08:12.009 --> 00:08:14.919
It wasn't she thought it was a good company
or she thought the management team over the

00:08:14.919 --> 00:08:18.830
long she just got out in order to avoid losing
money.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.120
And by the way, what's a stock buyback at
its core when a company buys back its own

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.120
stock?

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.560
The point is to pump the price back up.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:28.560
It is not.

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.419
Well, we feel so confident in our business
model that we are going to invest in our own.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:34.140
Come on, come on.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.100
That it would be cynical to think that's what's
going on.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:43.349
So there's a case here to be made for transaction
taxes, which eliminates this issue of are

00:08:43.349 --> 00:08:50.411
they genuine or cynical stock trades and why
would you expect anything else when this is

00:08:50.411 --> 00:08:52.820
the way that the stock market operates?

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:58.760
There's two sides to it, and I am as curious
as everybody else to see what happens with

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:57.360
this.

